Step 1: Check Docker Installation
Open Command Prompt or Terminal and enter:
docker --version
If Docker is installed correctly, its version will be displayed.
Example:
Docker version 28.x.x
Step 2: Create the Project Folder
Create a folder named:
student-management-api
Open the folder in Visual Studio Code.
You can also use:
mkdir student-management-api
cd student-management-api
code .
Step 3: Create the Flask Application
Create a file named:
app.py
Add the following code:
from flask import Flask, jsonify, request
app = Flask(__name__)
students = [
 {
 "id": 1,
 "name": "Rahul",
 "age": 20,
 "course": "Computer Science"
 },
{
 "id": 2,
 "name": "Priya",
 "age": 21,
 "course": "Information Technology"
 }
]
# GET API - Get all students
@app.route('/students', methods=['GET'])
def get_students():
 return jsonify(students)
# POST API - Add a new student
@app.route('/students', methods=['POST'])
def add_student():
 data = request.get_json()
 new_student = {
 "id": len(students) + 1,
 "name": data["name"],
 "age": data["age"],
 "course": data["course"]
 }
 students.append(new_student)
 return jsonify(new_student), 201
if __name__ == '__main__':
 app.run(host='0.0.0.0', port=5000, debug=True)
The host='0.0.0.0' allows the Flask application to be accessed from outside the Docker container.
Step 4: Create requirements.txt
Create a file named:
requirements.txt
Add:
Flask
This file specifies the Python package required by the application.
Step 5: Create a Dockerfile
Create a file named:
Dockerfile
Add the following code:
FROM python:3.11-slim
WORKDIR /app
COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt
COPY app.py .
EXPOSE 5000
CMD ["python", "app.py"]
Step 6: Build the Docker Image
Open the terminal inside the project folder and run:
docker build -t student-management-api .
The Docker command reads the Dockerfile and creates an image named:
student-management-api
To check the created image, run:
docker images
Expected output will contain:
student-management-api
Step 7: Run the Docker Container
Run the Docker image using:
docker run -d -p 5000:5000 --name student-api student-management-api
Here:
-d runs the container in detached mode.
-p 5000:5000 maps port 5000 of the host to port 5000 of the container.
--name student-api gives the container a name.
Check the running container using:
docker ps
Step 8: Test the Dockerized Application
Open a web browser and enter:
http://localhost:5000/students
Expected response:
[
 {
 "age": 20,
 "course": "Computer Science",
 "id": 1,
 "name": "Rahul"
 },
 {
 "age": 21,
 "course": "Information Technology",
 "id": 2,
 "name": "Priya"
 }
]
The Flask application is now running successfully inside a Docker container.
Step 9: Create a Docker Hub Account
Open Docker Hub in a web browser and create an account if you do not already have one.
After creating the account, note your Docker Hub username.
For example:
Username: yourusername
Step 10: Login to Docker Hub
Open the terminal and run:
docker login
Enter your Docker Hub username and password when prompted.
If login is successful, Docker will display a successful login message.
Step 11: Tag the Docker Image
Before uploading the image to Docker Hub, tag it using your Docker Hub username.
Run:
docker tag student-management-api yourusername/student-management-api:latest
Replace yourusername with your actual Docker Hub username.
For example:
docker tag student-management-api rahul123/student-management-api:latest
Step 12: Verify the Tagged Image
Run:
docker images
The output should contain an image similar to:
REPOSITORY TAG
yourusername/student-management-api latest
student-management-api latest
Step 13: Push the Image to Docker Hub
Run:
docker push yourusername/student-management-api:latest
Docker will upload the image to Docker Hub.
After the upload is completed, the image will be available in your Docker Hub repository.
Step 14: Pull the Image from Docker Hub
To verify that the image can be distributed, remove the local image if required and pull it again using:
docker pull yourusername/student-management-api:latest
The Docker image will be downloaded from Docker Hub.
Step 15: Run the Image from Docker Hub
Run the downloaded image using:
docker run -d -p 5000:5000 --name student-api-dockerhub yourusername/studentmanagement-api:latest
Check the running container:
docker ps
Then open:
http://localhost:5000/students
The Student Management API should be displayed.
